❌ FOUR MAJOR ERRORS
Errors
│
├── 1. Preprocessor Error
├── 2. Compile-Time Error
├── 3. Linker Error
└── 4. Run-Time Error
ERROR 1 — PREPROCESSOR ERROR
Description

A preprocessor error occurs while processing preprocessor directives.

Example
#include <unknown_header.h>

int main()
{
    return 0;
}
Command
gcc -E program.c -o program.i
Possible Error
fatal error: unknown_header.h: No such file or directory
Cause

The required header file cannot be found.

ERROR 2 — COMPILE-TIME ERROR
Description

A compile-time error occurs when the compiler finds invalid C syntax or an invalid C language construct.

Example
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int a = 10

    printf("%d\n", a);

    return 0;
}

The semicolon is missing.

Command
gcc -S program.c -o program.s

The compiler reports the error.

Correct Code
int a = 10;
ERROR 3 — LINKER ERROR
Description

A linker error occurs when the linker cannot find the definition of a required function or symbol.

Example
int main()
{
    display();

    return 0;
}

Here, display() is called but no definition is provided.

Command
gcc program.c -o program
Possible Error
undefined reference to `display'
Cause

The required function or symbol could not be resolved by the linker.

ERROR 4 — RUN-TIME ERROR
Description

A run-time error occurs after the program has successfully compiled and linked, while the executable is running.

Common Examples
Segmentation fault
Floating-point exception
Bus error
RUN-TIME EXAMPLE — SEGMENTATION FAULT
Program
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int *p = NULL;

    printf("%d\n", *p);

    return 0;
}
Compile
gcc program.c -o program
Run
./program
Possible Output
Segmentation fault
RUN-TIME EXAMPLE — FLOATING-POINT EXCEPTION
Program
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int a = 10;
    int b = 0;

    printf("%d\n", a / b);

    return 0;
}
Compile
gcc program.c -o program
Run
./program
On many Linux systems
Floating point exception
BUS ERROR

A bus error can occur because of an invalid memory access, including certain alignment violations depending on the processor and system.

The exact behavior depends on the processor and operating system.

